I cannot reproduce the outage from the CS lab to the Server Room. Layer-3 connectivity from the CS subnet (192.168.2.0/24) to the Server Room subnet (1.0.0.0/8) is working end-to-end:

- From multiple CS hosts (cs_hod, cs_lab1–4), ICMP to a server-room host pc1 (1.0.0.5) succeeds with 0% loss and low RTT. cs_lab1 also reaches router2’s server-side interface (1.0.0.1) with 0% loss.
- CS hosts have correct IP/gateway (e.g., cs_lab1: 192.168.2.3/24, default via 192.168.2.1). 
- Routers have consistent RIP routes: router0 learns 1.0.0.0/8 via router1 (10.10.0.2); router1 learns 1.0.0.0/8 via router2 (20.20.0.2); router2 has 1.0.0.0/8 directly connected. All relevant 192.168.x routes are present across r0–r2.

Mechanism: Because routing tables are correct and the CS hosts can ping both the server VLAN gateway and a server host, the path is intact. The reported “vanished” Server Room cannot be confirmed at the network layer now.

Localization: No faulty device or interface identified; r0–r2 control plane (RIP) and data plane are currently healthy.

Root cause: No active network fault found; the incident was likely transient or application/service-layer (e.g., file-share daemons) rather than a persistent routing/L2 issue.

Corrective fix: None required for the network. Please retry access to the file shares; if issues persist, provide target IP/ports so we can test TCP reachability, and check server-side services on 1.0.0.x. Consider enabling routing/service monitoring to capture any future transient events. 
